course: myths
lesson: 18
title: Vikings, knights and the Middle Ages
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Ned bursts into the classroom in his plastic helmet with two huge horns, roaring "Vikings wore these so enemies wet themselves!" And the Middle Ages of the story books are just as wild: everybody filthy, everybody dead at thirty, knights so heavy they were winched onto their horses by crane, and people who thought you'd fall off the edge of a flat Earth.
explain: Brilliant stories, and almost all made up later. The horned helmets came from costumes for an opera in 1876; no Viking-age helmet with horns has ever been found, and horns would just be something for an enemy to grab.
explain: Many of these myths were spread by later writers, especially in the 1800s, who liked to make the past look silly and backward. In fact medieval people washed, had public bathhouses, knew the Earth was round, and some noble women ran castles and even whole countries.
explain: Some myths hide in numbers: "everyone died at 30" is an average pulled down by the many babies who died. Work out the average of 1, 1, 60 and 70 and you get 33, yet two of those people lived to old age.
explain: The best answer is evidence from the time itself: a real helmet in a museum, a king's round golden orb in a painting, a modern person running in real armour. And sometimes the honest answer is "historians don't know", as with Robin Hood.
fact: No Viking-age helmet with horns has ever been found; the well-preserved Gjermundbu helmet is a rounded cap, and horned helmets came from costumes for an opera in 1876.
fact: Medieval people washed their hands and faces every day, and many towns had public bathhouses.
fact: Average life expectancy in the Middle Ages was low because many babies and children died; people who grew up often lived to their 50s, 60s or older.
fact: A full suit of battle armour weighed about 20 to 25 kilograms spread over the body, and knights could run, climb and get onto horses.
fact: In the old story, King Canute set his throne by the sea to show his flattering courtiers (the people around him who praised him to please him) that even a king cannot command the tide.
fact: Educated people in the Middle Ages knew the Earth was round; it was taught in universities and books, and kings held a round orb for the world.
fact: Historians do not know whether Robin Hood was one real person; the stories grew over centuries, and "giving to the poor" was added later.
fact: Medieval people drank weak ale, but they also drank water from wells and springs.
fact: The famous iron maidens were probably put together in the 1800s, and there is no good evidence they were used in the Middle Ages.
fact: Royal and noble women ran estates, managed money and wrote letters, and some ruled, like Æthelflæd, Lady of the Mercians, and Eleanor of Aquitaine.
fact: "Dark Ages" was a name used by later writers, mostly because fewer written records survive; it was a time of art and learning, like the Lindisfarne Gospels and the Sutton Hoo treasures.
exercise: Ned in his costume: "Vikings wore helmets with big horns!" What is true? | No Viking-age helmet with horns has ever been found | Every Viking warrior wore two horns | Only Viking kings wore horns | Vikings wore horns only at sea
exercise: Where did the horned Viking helmets come from? | Costumes for an opera in 1876 | Real helmets dug up in Norway | Drawings by Viking artists | Helmets that Vikings sold to the Romans
exercise: Why would horns be a bad idea on a battle helmet? | An enemy could grab them in a fight | They would make the helmet too light | They would scare away your own horse | They would melt in the sunshine
exercise: Imogen: "Medieval people never washed and were totally filthy!" What is true? | They washed daily, and towns had public bathhouses | They never washed at all, because they believed water spread illness | Only kings and queens were allowed to wash, and everyone else stayed dirty | They washed only once a year, at Easter, in the river near the church
exercise: Kai: "In the Middle Ages everyone died at about 30." What's the truth? | The average was pulled down by babies dying; many lived to 60 or more | Nobody ever lived past 30 | Everyone died on their 30th birthday | Adults died young from hard work, but babies and children usually lived long
exercise: Lottie: "Knights were so heavy they needed a crane to get on a horse!" What is true? | Armour weighed about 20 to 25 kg, and knights could run and mount | Armour weighed about a tonne | Knights had to be lifted by ten men | Knights never rode horses in armour
exercise: Harry's grandad: "King Canute was so big-headed he thought he could order the tide back." What does the old story say? | He did it to show courtiers that a king can't command the sea | He really thought the sea would obey him and got angry when it didn't | He made the tide go back by magic | He wanted to build a wall across the sea
exercise: Zoe: "Medieval people thought the Earth was flat." What is true? | Educated people knew it was round; it was taught in universities | Everybody believed it was flat until explorers sailed round it in about 1900 | Only sailors knew it was round, and they kept it a secret from the priests | Nobody had any idea about its shape, so maps simply stopped at the sea's edge
exercise: In old paintings, what does the king's round golden orb stand for? | The round world | A big golden apple | The Sun only | A ball for games
exercise: Theo: "Robin Hood was a real outlaw who robbed the rich for the poor." What's honest? | Historians don't know if he was one real person | He was real, and his house in Nottingham is still standing today | He was a real king of England who dressed up as an outlaw in the forest | He was made up for a Hollywood film in 1950 and never appeared in old songs
exercise: Ellie: "Medieval people drank beer all day because water was poison!" What is true? | They drank weak ale, but also water from wells and springs | They never drank a drop of water, because every well in the land was poisoned | All water in the Middle Ages killed people | Only children drank beer, never water
exercise: Noah: "Iron Maidens were used to torture people in the Middle Ages." What do historians think? | The famous ones were probably made in the 1800s for show | Every castle had one in the cellar, and they were used on thieves every week | They were invented by the Vikings and carried on longships to scare enemies | They were used in town squares across England until about a hundred years ago
exercise: Mia: "Medieval princesses just sat in towers waiting to be rescued." What's true? | Noble women ran estates and some ruled, like Æthelflæd | They were never allowed out of their rooms until they were married off | They only learned to sew and sing | They could not read or write at all
exercise: Oscar: "The Dark Ages were called dark because nothing happened and nobody could read." What is true? | It means fewer records survive; there was art and learning | Nobody in Europe could read or write for five hundred years, not even monks | There was hardly any sunlight in those years | Nothing at all was built in that time
exercise: Which treasure shows the "Dark Ages" weren't so dark? | The Sutton Hoo treasures | The Crown Jewels made last year | The Eiffel Tower | The Great Fire of London
test: An archaeologist digs up a real Viking helmet. What will it most likely look like? | A rounded metal cap without horns | A helmet with two curved bull's horns | A helmet with wings on the sides | A golden crown with spikes
test: Ages of four villagers who died: 1, 1, 60 and 70. Their average is 33. What does this show? | An average can hide that some people lived to old age | Everybody died at about 33 | Babies lived longer than adults | The sum must be wrong
test: "lol knights couldn't even walk in armour, they just fell over" What would museum tests show? | People in real armour can run, climb and get on horses | They can't move at all once it's on, so squires carried them about | They need a crane to stand up | They can only crawl slowly
test: Harry's grandad sticks to his version of Canute. How do you argue wisely? | Say kindly you read it the other way: he proved he couldn't | Tell him he's got it all wrong, then stop listening to anything else he says | Shout the right story louder until he gives up | Agree and then tell everyone he's wrong
test: A cartoon shows a medieval sailor scared of falling off the edge of the world. What should it show instead? | Educated people knew the Earth was round | Every sailor was terrified of the edge of the sea | Ships quite often sailed off the edge and were lost | The world really did have an edge back in those days
test: Theo: "Shut up, he's real, I've been to Sherwood Forest!" What's the honest reply? | The forest is real, but historians aren't sure he was | If the forest is real then he must be real too, that's how it works | He was real, but he lived in London | Nobody has ever been to Sherwood Forest
test: A fact book says water was poison so everyone drank beer, and you've read it in three books. Does that make it true? | No, being repeated isn't proof; they drank water too | Yes, three books can't be wrong | Yes, if it's printed it's always true | No, they drank only milk
test: A spooky museum leaflet shows an iron maiden "from 1350". What's a wise thought? | Historians think these were made much later for shows | It must be true, because museums are checked and can never be wrong | It was surely used every day in 1350 | It was made by Vikings for fun
test: What would a real medieval queen like Eleanor of Aquitaine have done all day? | Ruled lands, managed money and dealt with powerful people | Sat in a tower all day long, waiting for a knight to come and rescue her | Swept floors in the kitchen | Nothing, women weren't allowed to do anything
test: Ned is proud of his horned helmet costume. What's the kindest way to tell him the truth? | Say his costume's ace, then share that real ones had no horns | Laugh at him in front of the class | Grab the horns off his helmet | Tell him he's ruined Halloween
test: Your textbook calls them the "Dark Ages". Why should you be careful with that name? | A name can carry an opinion from later writers | It was literally dark all day then, because of thick clouds of smoke | The name was chosen by the people who were living in those very years | It proves that nothing at all happened in Europe for about five hundred years
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the helmets worn by warriors of the Viking age? | No helmet bearing horns from that age has been recovered | Helmets bearing horns were worn by every warrior of that age without exception | Helmets bearing horns were reserved for kings | Helmets bearing wings were worn at sea
exam: It is given that the average age at death in a medieval community was approximately thirty years. Identify the principal reason for this figure. | The average was depressed by the deaths of numerous infants and children | Adults customarily died upon attaining thirty years | No person survived beyond the age of forty | The figure was computed exclusively from soldiers
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the beliefs of educated persons in the Middle Ages regarding the shape of the Earth. | The Earth was understood to be spherical | The Earth was believed to be flat | The Earth was believed to be hollow | The shape of the Earth was unknown to all
exam: Pursuant to the account of King Canute and the tide, identify the purpose for which the throne was set upon the shore. | To demonstrate to flattering courtiers that a king cannot command the sea | To command the tide to recede by royal authority | To observe the arrival of an invading fleet | To bathe in the sea in the presence of the court
